00:18:25.000 --> 00:18:44.000
So I will introduce Verana. Some of you may know a little bit about this project, but I will try to be as to summarize so that it will be easy for you to understand and we can go straight to the governing point

00:18:44.000 --> 00:18:49.000
So the Verana is a registry of trust registries

00:18:49.000 --> 00:18:59.000
So, we believe that what we want is that any ecosystem should be able to create their own trust registry

00:18:59.000 --> 00:19:16.000
define a governance framework, publish the governance framework, define the list of credential schema they would like to use in their ecosystem and build a tree of accredited issuers

00:19:16.000 --> 00:19:31.000
Verifiers and holders. So this is the first brick of ARANA is public infrastructure where anyone can create its own ecosystem and define

00:19:31.000 --> 00:19:33.000
All of them.

00:19:33.000 --> 00:19:41.000
Then we have a second brick in Verana, which is the service identity

00:19:41.000 --> 00:19:53.000
We believe that in an Agentic internet, if there is no identity for AI agent, everybody's talking about that, but not only AI agents, any service, any connected thing

00:19:53.000 --> 00:19:59.000
We need to know who is the controller behind this entity

00:19:59.000 --> 00:20:18.000
And at the same time, if we are able to know who is a controller of an entity, then we can mutually authenticate before trying to connect. And then if we agree with the authentication, then we can connect and we can bootstrap

00:20:18.000 --> 00:20:34.000
protocol we want. So Vera defines some specifications based only on open standards. The verifiable trust specification that defines basically how to authenticate services

00:20:34.000 --> 00:20:57.000
And how do I authenticate software that is participating to connection in general? So not only services, but you can even use it in order to authenticate user agents, for example. So the principle is based on DID. Every service is identified by a DID is presenting some credentials

00:20:57.000 --> 00:21:14.000
In the DID documents, using linked verifiable presentations, and then the credentials are issued by some ecosystems that can be in Verana or could be in another registry of registry

00:21:14.000 --> 00:21:31.000
And at the end, we decide if we trust the service by deciding if we trust the ecosystem that issue the credentials. So the services identity is resolved like this in the Verana concept

00:21:31.000 --> 00:21:49.000
Because we are using a ledger to anchor ecosystems. So the ledger is used to store the trust registry of the ecosystem. So we store the schemas and we store the accreditation trees

00:21:49.000 --> 00:21:57.000
And obviously, no personal data, no information is stored in the ledger, only DIDs.

00:21:57.000 --> 00:22:15.000
In schemas. And then, because we have this ledger, each time there is a participant to an ecosystem, and each time these participant is presenting a public credential attached to the DID, then automatically we can build a graph

00:22:15.000 --> 00:22:26.000
So this graph is built by indexing every little service that is presenting credential from Ecosystem defined in Verana

00:22:26.000 --> 00:22:35.000
And it could be bridged to an ecosystem from other treasuries resources

00:22:35.000 --> 00:22:52.000
So at the end, using this trust graph, we can query this graph and, for example, request, ask where are the variable services of a company, for example, or show me all the agents

00:22:52.000 --> 00:23:10.000
that are certified ISO 123, and so on. So this is what we've built with Verana. There is a testnet currently working, and all the modules that are behind the ledger, the ledger is only a very small part

00:23:10.000 --> 00:23:16.000
of the infrastructure, but at the end, anyone can create a service

00:23:16.000 --> 00:23:35.000
And then publish a DID for this service with what they are calling in Europe a business wallet, but it's a cloud agent, let's say. And you attach… you join the ecosystem you want, you obtain credential, and you present the credentials in your documents

00:23:35.000 --> 00:23:53.000
You can be accredited by several ecosystem as an issuer, as a verifier. You can create your own ecosystem, etc. So, the idea that UDID, finally, is visible like that, the credentials they present

00:23:53.000 --> 00:24:00.000
And the accreditation that the UTID have.

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:18.000
So how do we manage the governance of that? By the way, I would say better how we plan to do it because one of the reason why we are presenting that to you is because it's not closed yet and we must

00:24:18.000 --> 00:24:28.000
receive feedback, and probably help. So we separated, we separated into entities

00:24:28.000 --> 00:24:44.000
the project. So we have the Verona Foundation that hosts a specification ends towards the open source software. So the Verana Foundation is only for the software and for the specification has no governance rights on the Verona infrastructure.

00:24:44.000 --> 00:24:49.000
And then from the other side, we have the Verana Council

00:24:49.000 --> 00:25:07.000
Which goal is to govern and secure the network. So we will focus this presentation today on the council because Verona Foundation is really for providing the software and the specifications

00:25:07.000 --> 00:25:09.000
So

00:25:09.000 --> 00:25:13.000
Because Verona is a registry of registries

00:25:13.000 --> 00:25:19.000
We have layered governance model

00:25:19.000 --> 00:25:27.000
The goal is to have every ecosystem have their own governance

00:25:27.000 --> 00:25:29.000
While we

00:25:29.000 --> 00:25:47.000
Governance for the infrastructure itself, what we are calling the network governance. And we want this network Governance to be as simple as possible and delegate all the sovereignty to the ecosystem that are building themselves.

00:25:47.000 --> 00:25:49.000
in the infrastructure

00:25:49.000 --> 00:25:59.000
So let's first talk about the Ecosystem Governance because it's simpler here in this presentation

00:25:59.000 --> 00:26:01.000
So

00:26:01.000 --> 00:26:16.000
Each ecosystem in Verana is 100% sovereign. So they define their own rules. They can define some business models for the credentials. They can explain

00:26:16.000 --> 00:26:34.000
How to join the ecosystem as an issuer, as a verifier. There are some fees for that. They can explain what are the risks, what are the rules, what are the sanctions, if you do not respect the governance framework as a participant

00:26:34.000 --> 00:26:48.000
And so on. So this is 100% sovereign, and the network governance cannot be part of the governance of each ecosystem. Each ecosystem is totally independent.

00:26:48.000 --> 00:26:59.000
So what we want is a public infrastructure where every ecosystem is sovereign. So that's why we have this separation

00:26:59.000 --> 00:27:20.000
Now, let's talk about the governance of the infrastructure. So if we want to give ecosystem a public infrastructure so they can create themselves as sovereign ecosystem then we need to define a governance for the network

00:27:20.000 --> 00:27:25.000
That is the more neutral possible. So

00:27:25.000 --> 00:27:28.000
What we want. We want neutrality

00:27:28.000 --> 00:27:44.000
Equality, so we don't want, for example, a member to control the governance of the infrastructure. We want openness, federalism, so that whole ecosystem are autonomous, transparency

00:27:44.000 --> 00:27:49.000
Accountability, data minimization

00:27:49.000 --> 00:27:55.000
And obviously the principle verified don't trust or don't trust verify

00:27:55.000 --> 00:28:02.000
And proportionality, in case of problems and legal safety.

00:28:02.000 --> 00:28:19.000
We don't want, we don't want someone to take over the governance of the council because of money, we don't want someone to take a position on the council for

00:28:19.000 --> 00:28:26.000
politic decisions. So we want something, the most neutral possible

00:28:26.000 --> 00:28:41.000
So, what we defined, and that is pending, it's just a draft, so it is pending a better definition, is a nonprofit Swiss association for the council

00:28:41.000 --> 00:28:50.000
Where we have a small boat with zero network authority, and we have one member, one vote

00:28:50.000 --> 00:29:05.000
And each member runs a ledger node, because finally the ledger is the only part that needs to be run by a selected council. All the other modules can be deployed and run by anyone.

00:29:05.000 --> 00:29:24.000
So we want the conceal to be a free membership, because we don't want any barrier based on money. Of course, we want regional balance, so we don't want all council members to be from the same region or from the same sector

00:29:24.000 --> 00:29:41.000
And what we don't want, we don't want capture, so we cannot have, for example, a single entity that has subsidiaries, and every subsidiary is member of the council, and at the end

00:29:41.000 --> 00:29:44.000
They are controlling the currency, so we don't want that.

00:29:44.000 --> 00:29:57.000
And we want to have public sector observers that can see what's happening in the country

00:29:57.000 --> 00:30:22.000
So concretely, in terms of what the Council does, of course they are building the Governance framework documents. That's the first topic, it's written here in small, but that's the first topic. They execute the nodes and

